4. METHOD -- how a request/response protocol is measured to STANDING-ORDERS §7
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
§7 says a traffic test crafts the exact frame the rule must match AND a control the rule must
NOT match, and counts both. For SNMPv3 that takes this shape, and every leg below obeys it:

  * the PERMITTED OID is the "must match" flow and the EXCLUDED OID is the control, read by
    the SAME user against the SAME engine in the SAME leg. A verdict never rests on one of
    them alone;
  * an INSTRUMENT CONTROL runs in every counted leg -- an ICMP exchange over the same link,
    or a read that must succeed -- so that a refusal is a measurement and not a dead path, a
    mangled credential or a locked-out user;
  * what no `show` command can establish -- whether the varbinds are really encrypted in
    flight -- is taken off the wire with tcpdump and decoded (section 6).

TWO NEW GENERIC TOOLS were written for this, in tools/ with README entries (never in work/):
  tools/snmpq.py    runs one net-snmp query and CLASSIFIES the outcome as
                    VALUE / NOSUCH / DENIED / AUTHFAIL / TIMEOUT / ERROR with a distinct exit
                    code, and grades it against `--expect`. net-snmp returns exit 1 for most
                    of those, so a raw exit code cannot grade a step.
  tools/snmpwire.py reads a libpcap file with no dependencies and reports, per SNMP message,
                    the version, the msgFlags auth/priv bits, the USM user name, the engine
                    ID, and whether msgData is a plaintext scopedPDU (ASN.1 SEQUENCE) or an
                    encryptedPDU (ASN.1 OCTET STRING) -- plus, for plaintext, the varbind OIDs
                    visible in the clear.

THE SHELL-QUOTING HAZARD, AND HOW IT WAS RULED OUT.  The case's passwords are
  auth md5  !#$%&'()*+,-./[\]^_`        (20 characters)
  priv des  :;<=@{}~0123456789ab        (20 characters)
`!`, `$`, backtick, `\`, `{}`, `~` and `'` are all live in a shell, and the host-side path to
snmpget crosses a local bash, ssh and a remote shell. A password eaten anywhere on that path
produces "Authentication failure", which reads exactly like the feature being broken. Three
things rule it out:
  (a) tools/tb shell-quotes every argument it sends and the tools take argv, never a shell
      string;
  (b) snmpq.py prints a non-disclosing FINGERPRINT of each password it actually received --
      `<PASS len=20 sha1=0eeef6a537>` for the auth password and `<PASS len=20 sha1=a587e0b9da>`
      for the priv password. Those two fingerprints are identical in every leg of this log,
      so the client used the same bytes throughout;
  (c) the decisive proof is behavioural: an SNMPv3 authPriv request that the agent ANSWERS
      WITH A VALUE has already passed the MD5 digest check and the DES decryption at the
      agent. Section 5 leg B and every later positive leg do exactly that. A mangled password
      cannot produce a value.
The agent side was recorded too: `show snmp-server user` shows the DUT stored the user as
md5/des under group ADMINadminADMINadmin, and `show running-config` shows the localized keys
`encrypted auth md5 0x071020b34855247c4708d62fbc81675a66 priv des
0x05f888ea77233b0d3b84169301d6500de2`.

