--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. WHAT WAS NOT EXERCISED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* NOTIFICATIONS / TRAPS. The authored config's `snmp-server host <manager> version 3 auth
  TESTtestTESTtestTEST` line WAS applied and WAS accepted, and it was removed at teardown.
  Trap DELIVERY was not exercised: none of the nine authored steps tests it, and it is
  outside the measurement plan for this case. It needs a receiver on UDP 162 (root on the
  box) and a trigger. NOT TESTED, deliberately, and cheap to add later -- the bench can do
  it (snmptrapd is installed).
* WRITE access. The group grants `write MOSTmostMOSTmostMOST`, which this case never uses;
  only reads were measured. NOT TESTED.
* SHA / AES. The case specifies md5 and des and those were used. `snmp-server user ... auth
  {md5|sha} ... priv {des|aes}` offers more and none of it was measured. NOT TESTED.
* The second device ("DUT2") of the authored Toplogy_A2 -- see deviation D2.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. FINDINGS AND OBSERVATIONS  (next free numbers: queue's Issues had F-11 / O-8 as the
   highest in use, so these start at F-12 and O-9)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
F-12  PRODUCT. An SNMPv3 GetNext whose successor OID falls outside the requesting user's MIB
      view receives NO RESPONSE AT ALL from this x960 (sin_x96056-20261012-24). RFC 3416
      §4.2.2 requires the varbind to be returned with the value endOfMibView. Measured with a
      5 s timeout and up to 3 retries, between two in-view GetNexts answered in milliseconds
      and two permitted GETs answered before and after, so it is not slowness, throttling or
      a dead agent. A GET of an out-of-view OID is handled correctly (noSuchObject); only
      GetNext is silent. CONSEQUENCE: `snmpwalk` against any view-restricted SNMPv3 user
      cannot terminate cleanly -- it stalls at the view boundary and ends in a client
      timeout, which an operator will read as an unreachable device.
      Evidence: work/s6-outofview-getnext.log, and the two walks in work/s5-steps4-5.log.

O-9   TEST CASE (not the platform). AWPTCM-T43870's authored precondition is internally
      inconsistent and cannot pass as written on this platform. The group reads view
      `MOSTmostMOSTmostMOST`; the block defines only a view called `system`; nothing ever
      defines `MOSTmostMOSTmostMOST`. Applied verbatim, EVERY read is refused and authored
      step 4 FAILS (work/s3-legA-verbatim.log). Supplying the one missing line
      `snmp-server view MOSTmostMOSTmostMOST 1.3.6.1.2.1.1 included` makes all nine steps
      behave exactly as authored. Secondary: the step-6 OID is typed `1.3.6.1.1.2.1.1.5`,
      which is not sysName; steps 7 and the case's own OID glossary say `1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5`.
      RECOMMENDED FIX to the case: rename the view in the config block to
      `MOSTmostMOSTmostMOST`, and correct the step-6 OID.

O-10  PLATFORM CLI. `snmp-server view <name> <oid> {included|excluded}` is per-OID, but its
      negation `no snmp-server view <name>` takes ONLY the view name and deletes the WHOLE
      view -- `no snmp-server view <name> <oid>` is rejected with `% Invalid input`
      (work/s21-qmark-no.log). There is no way to withdraw a single OID entry from a view;
      the view must be deleted and rebuilt. The same shape applies to `no snmp-server user
      <name>` and `no snmp-server group <name>`, both of which reject the fuller positive
      syntax. Worth knowing for any teardown that mirrors its setup line for line.

O-11  PLATFORM CLI, cosmetic. `show snmp-server user` has no column separator at maximum
      name length: 20-character names run together as
      `TESTtestTESTtestTESTADMINadminADMINadminmd5          des`. Unambiguous to a human who
      knows the field widths; not machine-parseable by whitespace.

O-12  PLATFORM, minor. An SNMPv3 message the agent cannot decrypt (correct auth password,
      wrong priv password) gets no response at all. RFC 3414 §3.2(8) expects
      usmStatsDecryptionErrors and a report PDU. The access outcome is correct (denied) and
      this is a common implementation choice, so it is recorded as an observation, not a
      defect. Same silence family as F-12. Evidence: work/s12-auth-bites.log E3c.

PLATFORM FACTS EARNED, for platforms/x960.md at wrap:
  - the bare `snmp-server` line is present at factory baseline on this build: the agent is
    ENABLED by default, with no community and no v3 user, so it answers engine discovery but
    nothing else;
  - `show snmp-server` defaults: Startup Trap Delay 30 s, SNMPv3 Authentication Max Fail 5,
    SNMPv3 Authentication Lockout Time 300 s. ** Any credential-failure limb must use ONE
    attempt per probe (`-r 0`) or the user is locked out for five minutes;**
  - engine ID (actual) is derived and survives a reload byte-for-byte; localized md5/des keys
    therefore survive with it;
  - `boot ?` offers only `config-file` and `system`; `no boot config-file` cleanly restores
    the implicit flash:/default.cfg pointer and leaves no residue in running-config;
  - an SNMPv2c request to an agent with no community is logged as
    `kern.err ... snmpd: Received SNMP Packet from illegal server <ip>`;
  - a group referencing an undefined view is accepted silently and denies everything.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
